g bathed and duly offered oblations of water to the gods and the manes of their ancestors, and performed Agnihotra* (Sacrifice with burnt offering.) sacrifices, and partaken of clarified butter like to nectar, those high-souled and auspicious ones, with glad hearts, sat down surrounding Viśvāmitra.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.857,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-8"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034009","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"46a3285aebec92c48196ef67e3052c86998c8c7f937c4bc9749b5e437cdcb6f2","compact":"c2fcb8d3f5ac005efc3a57e99bc3f7ba2eb3c001c7232f4f295a37a2d496eb03","nasal":"c8071623d24c68a7a75bc800cee4a4d51864b4a260207280b8804caf546b86e1"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-10","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":10,"english":"And the descendants of Raghu also sat down, occupying prominent places as befitted their rank. Then Rāma with a heart surcharged with cheerfulness spoke to Viśvāmitra, saying, O adorable one, I desire to hear how the Gangă flowing in three directions and embracing the three worlds, falls into the lord of streams and rivers.","alignment":{"type":"split","confidence":0.553,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-9","DUTT-1-35-10"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034010","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0c499519723f12378cc1dfecbcb1179c1c5de9303c4ee11468d440691df5a485","compact":"eeae24f18a7b78c022609ebcd38f91f60b3e715d77b2e22f3c20a3fe9d90a6ce","nasal":"e295b0c311188bf78fa2a870886d00055df52086ae53e437ac427f1e91ceb038"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-11","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":11,"english":"Influenced by Rama's speech, the mighty ascetic Viśvāmitra entered upon the history of the Gangā's origin and progress.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-11"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034011","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"c0efd645a0f153fc345dd4b3800816630a65174c4e1bd05615c1a05b776656c8","compact":"419a699ff348e0ecaa039490546f6ac90d06bb6ff2f4727117b576605d84bfec","nasal":"7fe8df953d4286a213e60d63101f3e91782b788daabba433cc732f2b71805f46"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-12","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":12,"english":"O Rāma, that great mine of ore, Himavat is the foremost of mountains. To him were born two daughters, unparalleled on earth in loveliness.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.873,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-12"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034012","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"051ded190ab21a354ce300b3559be1a41cc071a031e01f0fd953a416207ad954","compact":"0bcc3ca39c5ae2114bcf1a368cf34cdbbf76647ebd9b5ab8f16b9fd295faa88b","nasal":"3dc8146efb7e1ec64c5fd33dbd1cbd15f6d7f5465ca83ac7f69dad58b755fe96"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-13","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":13,"english":"O Rāma, their mother of dainty waist, the amiable daughter of Meru, named Menā, was the beloved wife of Himavat.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.869,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-13"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034013","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"268e65c2596392af31f77a8ceae814a526c0b40b33e9e3ec0ca4ad13c7c4af87","compact":"49dec10fc363ed1dc09f68d8623fca258b21e242b5710a377a9522dff52ef2ac","nasal":"49dec10fc363ed1dc09f68d8623fca258b21e242b5710a377a9522dff52ef2ac"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-14","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":14,"english":"She of whom was born Gangā the elder daughter of Himavat; and , O Raghava, a second daughter was also born to him, named Uma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-14"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034014","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"9dae3d8462f6fb5dc821982dbfcd9646133716d544cda83d852162b2380da318","compact":"4061c7eda82caf50a1ef546bb5a49c42ade2dee016932711dad2ce00bd5c2aa0","nasal":"54823f77b2c74b5d17e7b1a0c69a8893959d3ae8cc833b634f3b1427422a82dd"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-15","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":15,"english":"And it came to pass that once upon a time, the deities, with the view of accomplishing some work appertaining to them as divine beings, in a body besought that foremost of mountains for that river flowing in three worlds, Himavat in obedience to duty, conferred upon them his daughter flowing everywhere at will, and sanctifying all creatures. Thereat in the interests of the three worlds, accepting her, those having the welfare of the three worlds, at heart, went away with Ganga, considering themselves as having attained their desire.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.746,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034015","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"bc2cb1be0e0c512d78567da99837e948ca8bcf73b291123b5e200f01982c36dc","compact":"4a27780b85a6eb2a7db7559120656e359f3e2eaa86a8e94673785bcb15865785","nasal":"55703055934445c3ead88897b540bc2ba1aa34b5c6f443c285620533532656cb"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-16","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":16,"english":"And it came to pass that once upon a time, the deities, with the view of accomplishing some work appertaining to them as divine beings, in a body besought that foremost of mountains for that river flowing in three worlds, Himavat in obedience to duty, conferred upon them his daughter flowing everywhere at will, and sanctifying all creatures. Thereat in the interests of the three worlds, accepting her, those having the welfare of the three worlds, at heart, went away with Ganga, considering themselves as having attained their desire.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034016","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"3c43df90e2ed8de19ae4893a5a571ca4311e3da8cfb551505505eb24a57cc7af","compact":"6dee46982264459d91fdb04987f1ec87dbd54af1ac4e2c1de26c598082813acf","nasal":"7d08d03dbcd407bc3e01d3145e4f1da589aa3c4efd968b9f9d6869c14dfdb272"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-17","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":17,"english":"And it came to pass that once upon a time, the deities, with the view of accomplishing some work appertaining to them as divine beings, in a body besought that foremost of mountains for that river flowing in three worlds, Himavat in obedience to duty, conferred upon them his daughter flowing everywhere at will, and sanctifying all creatures. Thereat in the interests of the three worlds, accepting her, those having the welfare of the three worlds, at heart, went away with Ganga, considering themselves as having attained their desire.","alignment":{"type":"grouped","confidence":0.884,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-15"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034017","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"376f540d4f9130bc85f52e7606087b5c04400a7a64320ac9efcdac9166043a53","compact":"2e780696b298b0d462160d5406ee64919cecfd810adaed18ff6506cedfd44452","nasal":"8d089792ae3853fff89708a0fb69819cc6f54f5f53fad4aebb5c8a600a3c0028"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-18","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":18,"english":"The other daughter of the mountain, O descendant of Raghu, adopting a stern vow, began to carry on austerities, having asceticism for her wealth.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":0.864,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-16"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034018","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"1c8d3a2041504ddc7d482775ef1240e9325117c9b8598de01ece2fe1a175cef4","compact":"b766421efa5c0b38e32be97ef3eea0a2ee7d1d74ea9a89f97d21710a47d2cb61","nasal":"f0f7145c768977700226877ca6b17d111af14723d26379e3514bfbc31d2703b9"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-19","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":19,"english":"And that best of mountains bestowed upon Rudra of unequalled form his daughter Uma, furnished with fiery asceticism and worshipped of the worlds.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-17"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034019","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"effcb76e17ffcac9742e87f59afc401e2e38bf36c9ba932e3c3ebfd095b2002d","compact":"8550944a49c745121ca738cbf03cf8690673f7ad919593a89cfaacd1c8b919f8","nasal":"8550944a49c745121ca738cbf03cf8690673f7ad919593a89cfaacd1c8b919f8"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-20","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":34,"verse":20,"english":"And these, O Rāghava, are the daughters of that king of mountains, worshipped of all, viz, Gangā, the foremost of streams, and the divine Uma.","alignment":{"type":"1:1","confidence":1.0,"method":"shared-sanskrit-ngram","duttRefs":["DUTT-1-35-18"],"status":"unreviewed"},"source":{"sanskritLocator":"1034020","sanskritFile":"Ram01.txt","englishEdition":"M. N. Dutt 1891–94"},"sanskrit_fingerprints":{"exact":"0aedf1ef662cdd001d5c8f432b373753faf067ace533480534cc94c9d28c405d","compact":"f2a2b413c73a25d15ad2a9077aac4b90dd3fb575ce511fbd20cd0c1b5b34f380","nasal":"474bf837cda18d3ade6c31ce9f63991db687b6b2ba6700df424ddc10557083d0"}},{"id":"VRCE-1-34-21","kanda":1,"kandaId":"bala","sarga":